# Rust project-builder API

This directory owns the public Rust project-builder tutorial and its SDK
contracts. Read `creating-a-builder.mtf` for the complete path from a definition
to output, then the focused references below.

`@page.moth` owns website composition. It doesn't import this routing reference.
The examples use the `moth::builder` and `moth::host` public facades. Physical
crate placement doesn't change compiler, builder or runtime ownership.

## Reading routes

- Builder lifecycle and public API: `creating-a-builder.mtf`
- Source packages, Rust attributes, supported signatures, resources and context:
  `host-packages.mtf`
- Configuration, metadata directives, formatters, source adapters and import
  providers: `capabilities-and-directives.mtf`
- Assemblies, template fragments, entry errors, lifecycles, lowering, resources,
  output and testing: `assemblies-and-runtime.mtf`

## Shared authorities

`docs/compiler-design-overview.md` owns semantic compilation, identities,
result slots, validated compiler handoffs and the normal-root entry contract.
`docs/build-system-design.md` owns graph orchestration, package topology,
configuration, assembly, lifecycle inputs and output ownership.

Exact source directive contracts belong to
`docs/src/docs/directives/directives.mtf`. Language and memory references remain
authoritative for value semantics. This SDK supplies constrained registrations
and consumer services rather than an alternate source language.

## Boundary rules

The definition helpers produce one frozen capability surface. Public compiled
views borrow or reference their owning results without duplicating semantic
stores. Lowering services keep HIR, type environments, interners and analysis
side tables out of the ordinary builder API.

Rust host attributes generate checked descriptors and adapters over the same
manual binding path. Registration is explicit. Rust signatures use the closed
host profile, not guessed layouts or source generation. A host binding isn't a
network route or a portable browser implementation.

Normal-root startup preserves its success fragment slots and built-in Error!
channel. Publication waits for success and never implies rollback of prior IO.
Templates remain strings. Resource strings retain their structural origins
until a valid output context supplies their characters.
